

   =====( LastCallers V1.02 )=========================================


           Trion LastCallers CliDoor and/or Utility.

           (c) Copyright Paul Spijkerman, '96-'98. All rights reserved.

           Written for use with the Trion BBS package.


   ===================================================================


        This programm will show an ANSI LastCallers screen and is very
        configurable.  It uses the Lastcaller data in the Trion:data/
        directory so it doesn't have to scan the logs.
        You call it with the name and path of a configuration file.
        

        Like this:

        Lastcallers  doors:lastcallers/lastcallers.cfg

        Or:

        Lastcallers  lastcallers.cfg


        Note that the default config file path is "Doors:lastcallers/"
        You only have to specify the path of the configuration file if
        it is not in doors:lastcallers/


        There are 3 lastcaller configs suplied:

        *.NoCPS    .. Shows a lastcaller screen with transfered files.
        *.WithCPS  .. Compresses the info a little and adds baud rates.
        *.NoBytes  .. same as WithCPS but without transfered bytes,
                      so there is loads of space for name, place, CPS etc.


        To use it in a menu just add something like this to
        your menu.

Cmd "l"
   CliDoor  "doors:lastcallers/lastcallers lastcallers.cfg"
   more
EndCmd


        If you want to use it for a lastcallers screen you have
        to redirect the output of the programm to a file by adding
        a > character followed by a screen name and path.
        Something like this :

        Lastcallers  lastcallers.cfg >trion:text/login2.ansi


        Or something like this in your lastcallers (or logout) script:


doors:lastcallers/lastcallers lastcallers.cfg >trion:text/login2.ansi


        You can make as much configuration files as you want for
        different styles of screens.


        In the default BBS config there is a 'Lastcallers' script
        in the "Trion:scripts/" directory.
        In this script a lastcaller screen and a UsageGraph
        are made, the script itself is started from logout
        and incoming scripts.
        So if you want to change the way the lastcallers
        programm is started you only have to change one script.


        The configuration file is like in the C programming
        language style.
        Which means that you can use '%' codes for all the fields,
        where you put a %x code it is replaced by the value
        of that codes. Also you can put positive and negative
        numbers between the % and the character folowing it
        like in 'C' .. when you put a number between it
        it means that the field will be shown with the specified
        number of characters.
        If you give a negative number the characters will be
        shown at the left side of the field, if the number was
        positive the characters will be shown at the right side.

        The %a code is the name of the caller or system that
        called .. if you use %-20a in the config, then the
        name will be printed with enough spaces behind is so
        the complete field will be 20 characters.


        The %c code is the time a call took, if it was 5 minutes, then
        a   %c code will be translated to "5"    in the screen
        a  %4c code will be translated to "   5" in the screen
        a %-4c code will be translated to "5   " in the screen

        These are the supported characters:


   %n   =  Number of node the call was on.                   (UWORD)
   %a   =  Name of user/remote sysop/fax or voice received.  (STRING)
   %b   =  Place of user/remote nodenumber                   (STRING)
   %c   =  Time of call (5 characters)                       (STRING)
   %d   =  Number of Minutes the call lasted.                (ULONG)

   %e   =  Number of received bytes (4 characters)           (STRING)
   %f   =  Units of received bytes  (K or M or space)        (STRING)
   %g   =  number of files received (2 characters)           (STRING)

   %h   =  Number of sent bytes (4 characters)               (STRING)
   %i   =  Units of sent bytes  ('k' or 'k' or space)        (STRING)
   %j   =  number of files sent (2 characters)               (STRING)

   %p   =  Sysop Paged flag  (1 character '.' or 'P')        (STRING)
   %q   =  Commented flag    (1 character '.' or 'C')        (STRING)
   %r   =  New user flag     (1 character '.' or 'N')        (STRING)
   %s   =  Carrier lost flag (1 character '.' or 'L')        (STRING)

   %t   =  Connect speed in bits per second ..k. (4 chars)   (STRING)


